Delete Kth Node — Revision

To delete the kth node of a singly linked list, first handle the edge cases: if the list is empty, return null; if k == 1, move head to head.next because the head itself must be deleted. Otherwise, start a current pointer at head and move it to the node just before the kth node. Then delete the kth node by setting current.next = current.next.next, which skips the kth node and connects the previous node directly to the next node. Finally, return head.

Time: O(n) — we may traverse the list up to the kth node.
Auxiliary Space: O(1) — only the current pointer is used.

CODE:
======================================

import java.util.*;

class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        data = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) {
        data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthNode(ListNode head, int k) {

        // If the list is empty
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // If k = 1, delete the head node
        if (k == 1) {
            head = head.next;
            return head;
        }

        // Start from the head
        ListNode current = head;

        // Move current to the node just before the kth node
        for (int i = 1; i < k - 1; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        // Delete the kth node
        // current -> kth node -> next node
        // current -> next node
        current.next = current.next.next;

        // Return the head of the modified list
        return head;
    }
}
